<div class="draftbar">Value Chains — a research layer of the Critical Materials Atlas</div>
<header class="topbar"><div class="wrap"><a class="wordmark" href="../"><span class="mark"></span>Critical Materials Atlas</a><nav class="topnav"><a href="../explorer">Explore</a><a href="../value-chains">Value chains</a><a href="../analysis">Research</a><a href="../method">Method</a><a href="../data">Download data</a></nav></div></header>
<main id="main">
<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Heat pumps · Components and assembly</div>
  <h1>$title</h1>
  <p class="deck">$deck</p>
  <p class="byline">Evidence review · <time datetime="$date">25 November 2026</time> · <a href="PRODUCTION.md">Claim register</a></p>
</div></section>
<article>
  <p>The European Commission reports spare heat-pump assembly capacity, especially for hydronic units, alongside gaps in compressor and four-way valve production. The IEA finds that rotary compressor capacity is highly concentrated globally, while scroll compressor capacity is more geographically diverse. The question is how much factory output a disruption would put at risk for a line that needs a specific compressor. $commission2026. $rotary.</p>

  <p class="note"><a href="#second-review">Follow-up on capacity and delayed deliveries</a></p>

  <h2>Assembly, component supply and installation</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Heat-pump evidence by stage">
    <table>
      <caption>Technology and measurement scope</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Evidence used</th><th scope="col">Scope</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Materials</th><td>JRC materials inventory</td><td>Steel and copper are major inputs. Some motors use permanent magnets. Material coefficients vary by design.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Components</th><td>Global capacity by compressor type, EU component assessments and supplier disclosures</td><td>Rotary and scroll figures pool multiple applications. A factory requires specific compressors, drives, refrigerants and ratings.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Assembly</th><td>IEA global and JRC EU estimates</td><td>Heat pumps for buildings and a separate mixed-technology EU benchmark. The model covers a single residential air-to-water design.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Deployment</th><td>IEA workforce evidence and EU regulation</td><td>Installation and servicing depend on local skills, building conditions, demand and operating economics.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The JRC inventory identifies copper and, in some designs, neodymium in the brushless DC motors that some heat pumps use. It describes generally limited material-related vulnerabilities and identifies risks from component disruptions and metal prices. Establishing a particular raw-material constraint would require material demand and available supply by grade and application. $jrc.</p>
  <p>The Kigali Amendment phases down HFCs globally. EU Regulation 2024/573 sets separate rules for placing equipment on the EU market. For example, Annex IV limits fluorinated gases with a global warming potential of 150 or higher in split air-to-water units up to 12 kW from January 2027, subject to safety exceptions and specific derogations. Different refrigerants can require different compressors, controls and safety provisions. Trained technicians are a deployment constraint documented separately by the IEA. $kigali. $regulation. $derogation. $monitor.</p>

  <h2>Capacity depends on the product and the year</h2>
  <p>The IEA estimates global manufacturing capacity for building heat pumps at about $world_gw GW per year in 2024. More than $local_share% of sales were supplied locally in each major market. Local sourcing refers to the finished equipment and gives no breakdown of component origins. $monitor.</p>
  <table class="numbers">
    <caption><strong>Source estimates.</strong> Shares of global heat-pump manufacturing capacity in 2024</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Location</th><th scope="col">Share</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$region_rows
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The Commission’s 2025 report judged EU final assembly on track for its anticipated 2030 needs if planned expansions materialised. The 2026 industry assessment found underused hydronic assembly capacity and deficits in compressor and four-way valve manufacturing. These findings describe different periods and stages, and they count capacity differently: the 2025 report puts existing EU final-assembly capacity at about 24 GW thermal in 2023, against the JRC’s 77 GW. $commission2025. $commission2026.</p>
  <p><strong>Calculated comparison.</strong> The JRC’s 2025 mixed-technology EU estimate was $nameplate_count million units per year. At its assumed $assumed_kw kW per unit, this is $eu_nameplate_gw GW of thermal nameplate output per year. Holding that estimate fixed, it covers about $flat_ratio of the rounded $flat_gw GW annual requirement in the JRC’s flat path and $growth_ratio of the $growth_gw GW requirement in 2030 on its growth path. The two deployment paths are modelled. Meeting an annual average leaves open whether capacity is sufficient in the peak year. These JRC quantities use their own technology coverage and conversion. $jrc.</p>

  <h2>Qualified compressors can limit assembly</h2>
  <p>The IEA locates about $rotary_share% of global rotary compressor manufacturing capacity in China in 2024. The category includes applications such as air conditioning. Its chart shows more geographically dispersed scroll capacity. Neither category isolates compatible volumes for EU residential air-to-water lines. $rotary.</p>
  <p>Copeland reports that a Weishaupt residential air-to-water design uses its variable-speed R290 scroll compressor with a qualified drive and controller. A separate Copeland–Daikin announcement describes a swing rotary and controls offering for European residential heat pumps. These disclosures identify different component configurations and give no qualified delivery volumes for the disruption case. $copeland. $joint.</p>

  <h2>When a compressor interruption cuts factory output</h2>
  <p><strong>Modelled.</strong> The illustration follows one EU-27 residential air-to-water line over $months months. All numbers in the stress test are assumptions or calculations from them. Monthly orders are $monthly_demand units and assembly can make $monthly_assembly. Normal monthly arrivals are $normal_domestic_receipts domestic and $normal_import_receipts imported compressors. The design uses $compressors_per_unit compressor per unit. For $shock_months months the example removes $shock_cut_pct% of the $exposed_import_receipts imports on an assumed China-origin route, cutting regular arrivals by $lost_imports_per_month per month. The line starts with $opening_stock compressors in stock. Alternatives can supply $replacement_capacity_per_month per month after $qualification_delay_months full months of qualification. All other inputs are assumed available.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Heat-pump intervention comparison">
    <table class="numbers">
      <caption><strong>Modelled.</strong> Factory output and unmet orders over $months months, in heat-pump units</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Change to the interruption case</th><th scope="col">Output</th><th scope="col">Unmet orders</th><th scope="col">Units lost to disruption</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$scenario_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Additional assembly capacity leaves the loss at $assembly_loss units in this case. Additional compatible capacity reduces losses after it qualifies. Prequalified replacement or larger stocks eliminate the shortfall on their own under the displayed assumptions. The comparison concerns physical output. Ranking these options by cost would require prices, lead times and product approvals.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Monthly stock and delivery balance for the interruption case</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Monthly compressor balance">
      <table class="numbers">
        <caption><strong>Modelled.</strong> Compressor counts and heat-pump units, by month</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Total compressor receipts</th><th scope="col">Of which replacements</th><th scope="col">Stock draw</th><th scope="col">Closing stock</th><th scope="col">Units made</th><th scope="col">Unmet orders</th></tr></thead><tbody>
$month_rows
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Sensitivity to inventory and qualification time">
    <table class="numbers">
      <caption><strong>Modelled.</strong> Heat-pump units lost to the shock as opening inventory and qualification delay change</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Opening compressors</th>$delay_head</tr></thead><tbody>
$loss_grid
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>With $opening_stock compressors in stock, the grid shows how earlier qualification can cover a temporary gap. The downloadable results also vary qualified replacement from $min_replacement to $max_replacement compressors per month and orders from $min_orders to $max_orders units per month. Across that full set the loss against a matched no-shock case ranges from $low_loss to $high_loss units. High demand can cause unmet orders even in the no-shock case. The range describes the chosen scenarios and assigns no probabilities. Each month’s balance counts stock once and lets unchanged arrivals resume after the shock.</p>
  <p>Calibrating the model would require the component mix by design, actual procurement by origin, opening stocks, qualified spare capacity and approval timelines. Production and unmet factory orders are the outputs. Installed heat pumps and heating delivered additionally depend on local installers, demand and building conditions.</p>

  <h2>Finished-equipment customs trade</h2>
  <p>The checked-in BACI series measures nominal cross-border value in HS 841861, complete heat pumps excluding machines of heading 8415. That description applies from 2017. For 2002–2016 the archive uses the HS 2002 edition, where the same code covers compression-type units whose condensers are heat exchangers, so the earlier years are not strictly comparable. Mixed-use compressor and air-conditioning categories require separate product evidence. This extract gives no compressor provenance or compatibility information. $baci.</p>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="control" id="static-trade" hidden><label for="trade-slider">Trade year</label><input id="trade-slider" type="range" min="0" max="0" value="0"></div>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="World exports of finished heat pumps">
      <table>
        <caption><strong>Reported customs trade.</strong> BACI V202601 · <span id="trade-year" aria-live="polite">$trade_year</span></caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Traded category</th><th scope="col">World export value</th><th scope="col">Leading exporter</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody id="trade-body">
        <tr data-trade-code="$trade_code"><th scope="row">$trade_title<br><span class="note">HS $trade_code</span></th><td>USD $trade_value billion</td><td>$trade_lead</td><td>$trade_boundary</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="second-review">
    <h2>Follow-up on capacity and delayed deliveries</h2>
    <p class="note"><time datetime="$review_date">25 November 2026</time></p>
    <p>The model illustrates how stock, supplier approval and compatible components affect one factory line during an assumed interruption. Plant purchasing records and qualified spare capacity would be needed to estimate an actual shortfall.</p>

    <h3>How the EU capacity estimates were made</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="EU heat-pump capacity estimates by source">
      <table>
        <caption>Published estimates of EU final assembly capacity</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Source and year measured</th><th scope="col">Existing capacity</th><th scope="col">Coverage and conversion</th><th scope="col">Announced capacity</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
$capacity_crosswalk
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The public sources do not give a common plant list and a common conversion to thermal GW. The gap between the estimates cannot be apportioned to expansion, technology mix or assumptions. The comparison of $eu_nameplate_gw GW with $flat_gw GW and $growth_gw GW above uses the JRC study’s own assumptions for both assembly capacity and deployment. $commission2025. $jrc_followup.</p>

    <h3>The compressor in the follow-up case</h3>
    <p>The hypothetical EU-27 residential air-to-water line uses $compressor_configuration. A supplier disclosure documents a residential system with this configuration. The scenario assumes exposure to a China-origin import route. The IEA’s share for global rotary capacity gives no sourcing or availability estimate for this scroll design. Actual purchases, inventories and approval times are unknown. $copeland. $rotary.</p>

    <h3>When delayed orders can be filled</h3>
    <p><strong>Modelled.</strong> The follow-up keeps the original disruption and orders for the first $months months. All cases deliver $delivered_initial units in that period. Unfilled orders carry forward. Monthly new orders stay at $monthly_demand. From month $recovery_first to month $recovery_last, the cases vary extra qualified deliveries above the restored $regular_receipts regular monthly receipts. The cancellation case removes $example_cancellations orders at the end of month $months. Each completed unit counts as one delivery.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Modelled deliveries and backlog">
      <table class="numbers">
        <caption>Modelled deliveries, cancellations and backlog, in units</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Extra compressors per month</th><th scope="col">Cancelled orders</th><th scope="col">Peak month-end backlog</th><th scope="col">Delivered by month $recovery_last</th><th scope="col">Backlog at month $recovery_last</th><th scope="col">Month backlog clears</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
$recovery_rows
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>In the case without cancellations, $initial_backlog orders remain at the end of month $months. Regular receipts cover new orders, so the backlog persists when those receipts resume. Clearing it by month $recovery_last with a constant extra supply would require $required_extra additional compatible compressors and enough headroom to assemble $recovery_rate units per month. The assumed line can assemble $monthly_assembly. Orders still open at the end of the model may be filled later. Only the cancellation case explicitly removes orders. The <a href="out/heat_pump_chain.json">evidence JSON</a> gives the monthly balance for each case.</p>
  </section>

  <h2>Evidence and reproduction</h2>
  <p>The <a href="PRODUCTION.md">claim register</a> records how the pilot’s claims were checked and how this analysis relates to the IEA and JRC studies. The <a href="README.md">methods and commands</a>, <a href="evidence_inputs.json">inputs</a>, <a href="out/heat_pump_chain.json">calculated evidence</a> and <a href="out/heat_pump_trade.json">trade data</a> are available for reproduction.</p>
</article>
